    "result_text": "No stale references. The change is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI made the `householdId` deterministic and reworked the household rules to key off it, keeping the change small and confined to low-complexity units.\n\n**What changed (3 files):**\n\n1. **`Households.java`** (rewritten \u2014 the low-complexity home for the logic):\n   - `idFor(owner)` \u2014 computes the household id as the first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode`, so owners with the same last name and postcode share it automatically.\n   - `isDuplicate(...)` \u2014 now flags a same-household owner as a duplicate (\u2192 409) unless `sharesHousehold` is `true`, which only *bypasses* the block (no more link-stamping side effect).\n   - `size(...)` \u2014 household size (existing housemates + 1), computed from the shared `householdId`.\n   - The old `joinHousehold` (which minted a UUID and re-saved housemates) and the address-based key are gone.\n\n2. **`PossibleDuplicates.java`** \u2014 `matchId` now takes `sharesHousehold` and returns `null` when it's `true`, so a *declared* household member is never flagged as a suspected duplicate.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 four small edits: stamp the deterministic `householdId` right after mapping (so identity/household checks and the DTO read it), add one household-duplicate `if` returning `CONFLICT`, set `householdMemberCount` from `Households.size`, and pass `sharesHousehold` through to `PossibleDuplicates.matchId`.\n\n**Behavioral result** matching the spec: a second owner with the same last name + postcode is rejected 409 unless it sets `sharesHousehold`, in which case it's created as a declared member and not flagged as a possible duplicate; duplicate detection and household size both key off the computed `householdId`.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
